#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096346 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bf804d6b-ceba-558b-8cde-11d70906372f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3739" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8).$ At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/54d2fe17d8d36a23c7c714242ffbfa12a56fd8e870b7672f1ce9b7e02f6508b4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=4$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=4,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=6$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x = 6,$  the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the points of inflection are $x=0$ and $x=4$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096347 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "803d8981-7cc1-5f4a-bb47-21d7583e00ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-3742" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/6b146f4a7bec69f1e984b3e2aa0e3c38ea65c4464da037759b41eac2fd97325f.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative $f''(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of $f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y=f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x = -4$ and $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-4$ and $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096348 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/id #uuid "ac3b585a-909a-5498-8c39-d8d03e378d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-3746" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection (if any) for $f(x) = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(x) & = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22\\\n \t\tf'(x) & = 3x^2 + 18x + 24 \\\n \t\tf''(x) & = 6x + 18\n}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t6x+ 18 &= 0\\\n \t\t6x  &= -18\\\n \t\tx &= -3.\n}\n$$\n\nFinally,  we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3.$ So we pick two points around it, say,  $x=-4$ and $x=-2$, and compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-4) & =6(-4)+18=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =6(-2)+18=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096349 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/id #uuid "ed7d94d2-25c5-53d9-830e-a659d304ecb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3747" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $f(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 + 6 x^2.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\qquad$ $x=0 \\quad$ and $\\quad x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-24(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-24(2)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a329ca-e000-513c-a624-fe32c9f29396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093760} {:answer/id #uuid "0774774a-5064-5ba7-8b6d-ab0d82cb1f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093761} {:answer/id #uuid "1f819151-c5cf-5b4d-8903-0cab4d515534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093762} {:answer/id #uuid "2141b62a-7e3d-59eb-9feb-9ef7a74970db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093763} {:answer/id #uuid "56b4071c-cfd6-5184-aa61-e04e7eeb99b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56b4071c-cfd6-5184-aa61-e04e7eeb99b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093764} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a4b055-23cf-5535-b288-ddb6c6e34d53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c163d924-6190-52ae-9f3b-628d336cfc56" :question/math-academy-id "q-114407" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-16x+26) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-10x+10) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ $\\,x=1,\\,$ and $\\,x=3,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}-2(-1)^{2})=-3{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}-2(1)^{2})=-e<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{3}((3)^{3}-2(3)^{2})=9{e}^{3}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2,$ we conclude that $x=2$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only $x=2$ is an inflection point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e90ca144-d79e-50cf-8d47-03c0479ec34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095031} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8df573-c13f-5589-a3e4-38a7fd4afa0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095032} {:answer/id #uuid "556a65ca-a4a0-5263-b495-f4edf0b6d408" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095033} {:answer/id #uuid "63c5c382-e954-57c7-9619-2fda2a9a84f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095034} {:answer/id #uuid "7a098f52-985a-50ea-baa8-722f1048e871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63c5c382-e954-57c7-9619-2fda2a9a84f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095034} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5dd35d-30e6-5b53-bd0c-23c6810c7491" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095030}] :question/id #uuid "4c4b4867-1510-53f9-af59-23034ea3f47c" :question/math-academy-id "q-20850" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $g(x) = x^{4} - 4x^{3} + 6x^{2} - 2x$." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a59a296c-f26b-57d2-9f66-3089d690018b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-6$ and $x =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095185} {:answer/id #uuid "448169d1-793a-5de6-ab93-2ab928cd2db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-4$ and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095186} {:answer/id #uuid "4959333a-5e8c-57af-a996-2eb528af419a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095187} {:answer/id #uuid "645b2ac0-0d2d-51c4-983b-5d9739a24c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095188} {:answer/id #uuid "7efb67cf-1659-5361-bf87-0b82d0524c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-4$, $x = 0$, and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "645b2ac0-0d2d-51c4-983b-5d9739a24c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ and $x = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095188} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1936dc6-d067-5933-92a7-774acfe3f044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095184}] :question/id #uuid "05af1cea-c88c-5fcc-8838-0055bd7f29aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-31213" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1046/Source/Images/q-31213.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$. The graph $y = f^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "306164fa-4dd6-5c1a-a107-b6af8267d7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294017} {:answer/id #uuid "66acfaac-58a5-5185-a6d3-c3d0d2c80a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294018} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e389c4-06ed-5dc1-8e71-6f3093c74722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294019} {:answer/id #uuid "09b2ac1d-4454-5f3f-98b2-c98690ed692a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294020} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf65c2b-bc08-5529-b64a-1f85068785ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6,\\,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "306164fa-4dd6-5c1a-a107-b6af8267d7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294017} :answer-field/id #uuid "98b053f5-2909-537c-919b-b0d0481d82f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98bca65a-5215-5c3c-9419-26f83fa4001a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31219" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/5d776a61d1039639d91e2c667c68965d3e2c901cdc926e8c54e6356cf1d87c2e.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$ \n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244a8ea8-991a-5546-a01e-f67f4b8c06c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "244a8ea8-991a-5546-a01e-f67f4b8c06c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294040} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3f11e8f-09c4-58ef-846e-f3450b62633c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6de3588d-45bf-5afc-a746-6941c52fd3d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344063" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2+\\frac{1}{x-2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+3-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2+\\frac{2}{(0-2)^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =2+\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{3}{2}-2)}^{3}}=-14<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0855285-378e-5c34-b35e-85aabd09a19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0855285-378e-5c34-b35e-85aabd09a19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294055} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7c0c38c-7dd0-5344-bcb5-cf9e5fb0e6d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "45f9354a-20d2-5917-8cc8-23e78cc4519e" :question/math-academy-id "q-344246" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-6x+6) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3})+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $\\,x=-1,\\,$ and $\\,x=1,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & ={e}^{-4}((-4)^{3}+3(-4)^{2})=-16{e}^{-4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2})=2{e}^{-1}>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}+3(1)^{2})=4e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is an inflection point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065deb0c-5d57-546a-8c8d-c914f8b17320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095786} {:answer/id #uuid "2b03460f-477c-5b1d-b368-fe255ea85a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095787} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0c35f0-de90-5a67-88a5-46ccd8b6d36d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ and $x = 5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095788} {:answer/id #uuid "532d9d26-989b-5702-a201-7fe2e4b78002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095789} {:answer/id #uuid "54c1cb8b-f690-5b80-b7db-a62fed7b70bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c1cb8b-f690-5b80-b7db-a62fed7b70bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095790} :answer-field/id #uuid "19b35f28-2017-55f5-b4dc-61220e3e94f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2792f399-d353-50d3-affd-f9260fbd9716" :question/math-academy-id "q-51428" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/a8fadcc272d96492194b2d46f1ae66151fb4fd16b2ee42a6840ff335aa0b4262.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where \n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed: \n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=3$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=3.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=5$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=5.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only."}]}
